The code statements that lead to the bug are:
bo = xe_bo_alloc();
...
attach = dma_buf_dynamic_attach(dma_buf, dev->dev, attach_ops, &bo->ttm.base);
...
obj = xe_dma_buf_init_obj(dev, bo, dma_buf);
Reason:
The bug occurs because the dma-buf attachment is created via `dma_buf_dynamic_attach()` before the buffer object is fully initialized by `xe_dma_buf_init_obj()`. This exposes an empty or partially initialized `bo` to the exporter's `invalidate_mappings` callback, which can be triggered at any time once the attachment is added to the global list. Additionally, if `xe_dma_buf_init_obj()` fails after attachment, the old code calls `xe_bo_free(bo)` but the object may still be referenced by the exporter, leading to a use-after-free race.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
obj = xe_dma_buf_init_obj(dev, bo, dma_buf);
if (IS_ERR(obj))
	return obj;
...
attach = dma_buf_dynamic_attach(dma_buf, dev->dev, attach_ops, &bo->ttm.base);
if (IS_ERR(attach)) {
	obj = ERR_CAST(attach);
	goto out_err;
}
...
out_err:
	xe_bo_put(bo);
Reason:
The fix moves `xe_dma_buf_init_obj()` before `dma_buf_dynamic_attach()`, ensuring the buffer object is fully initialized before it becomes globally visible via the attachment list. This prevents the exporter from calling into `invalidate_mappings` on an empty or partially initialized `bo`. Additionally, after successful init, the error path uses `xe_bo_put(bo)` instead of `xe_bo_free(bo)` because `xe_dma_buf_init_obj()` takes ownership of the raw bo; if the subsequent attach fails, `xe_bo_put` correctly drops the reference without double-freeing.

